Hardware: L40S (CUDA 12.x), single-card.

Summary
-------
Correctness when DEER converges: max |par - seq| in {1e-6 ... 1.5e-4} for T <= 1024.
Iterations scale roughly LINEARLY with T:
    T   iters
    128  111
    256  168
    512  364
   1024  716
Past T=1024 the solver hits max_iter=1000 and the trajectory has not
converged (state error ~ 2-3 in absolute value at convergence boundary
particles).

No wall-clock speedup at any T.  At T=128 the parallel solver is already
~6x slower than sequential; ratio worsens with T.

Diagnosis
---------
Linear iters-vs-T scaling means each DEER Newton step propagates ancestor
information by only O(1) timesteps.  This is the genealogy chain being
fundamentally serial: when a child ancestor flips at time t, the
downstream trajectory must re-thread, and the soft Gumbel-Softmax
surrogate's gradient is only locally accurate (one flip per step).

This is structurally different from MALA accept/reject: MALA's discrete
decision is local to a single chain (which value to keep) and does not
change the chain's identity; PF resampling changes child particle
identity, coupling all particles.  The soft Jacobian smears the
coupling but does not collapse it into log T parallel work.

Implication for next iteration
------------------------------
Construct must avoid resampling at most timesteps.  Twisted SMC keeps
ESS high so resampling triggers rarely; combining twisted-PF with
Gumbel-STE resampling at occasional checkpoints isolates the discrete
events to a small number of timesteps, while between checkpoints each
particle's chain is smooth and DEER-friendly.
